# UI Sleuth environment configuration
#
# Copy to .env in the directory you run `ui-sleuth` from (the .env is looked up from
# the current working directory, once per CLI invocation):
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Proxies are strictly opt-in: nothing below is used unless a proxy option is enabled,
# either by a CLI flag or by one of the UI_SLEUTH_* switches in section 1.
# Precedence: CLI flags > UI_SLEUTH_* (env/.env) > direct connection.
# If any proxy flag is given on the command line, all UI_SLEUTH_* proxy settings are ignored
# for that run.
#
# Accepted proxy formats (everywhere a proxy string is read):
#   scheme://user:pass@host:port   (http, https, socks5, ...)
#   user:pass@host:port
#   host:port:user:pass            (Decodo dashboard export format)
#   host:port
# Credentials are always redacted in console output and logs.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Proxy switches (mirror the CLI flags; enable at most one source)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Single proxy                                      (same as --proxy)
# UI_SLEUTH_PROXY=user:pass@isp.decodo.com:10001

# File with one proxy per line, # comments allowed  (same as --proxy-file)
# UI_SLEUTH_PROXY_FILE=proxies.txt

# Use the Decodo settings in section 2              (same as --decodo)
# Errors out (exit 1) if no usable Decodo configuration is found.
# UI_SLEUTH_DECODO=1

# Use PROXY_FILE / HTTPS_PROXY / HTTP_PROXY (section 3) (same as --proxy-env)
# UI_SLEUTH_PROXY_ENV=1

# Force a direct connection                        (same as --no-proxy)
# Cannot be combined with another UI_SLEUTH_* proxy switch.
# UI_SLEUTH_NO_PROXY=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Decodo ISP proxies (read only with --decodo or UI_SLEUTH_DECODO=1)
#    Resolution order: DECODO_PROXY_URL, then USERNAME+PASSWORD pool, then DECODO_PROXY_FILE.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Option A: one fixed endpoint
# DECODO_PROXY_URL=http://user:pass@isp.decodo.com:10001

# Option B: rotating pool across a port range (one proxy per port, inclusive)
DECODO_USERNAME=
DECODO_PASSWORD=
# DECODO_HOST=isp.decodo.com
# DECODO_PORT_START=10001
# DECODO_PORT_END=10010

# Option C: file of Decodo proxies (any accepted format, one per line)
# DECODO_PROXY_FILE=decodo_proxies.txt

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2b. Secondary Decodo endpoint (read only with --decodo; needs a section 2 primary)
#     Own host, credentials and port range. All three of HOST/USERNAME/PASSWORD are
#     required once any DECODO_SECONDARY_* is set (otherwise exit 1, values never shown).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# DECODO_SECONDARY_HOST=203.0.113.7
# DECODO_SECONDARY_USERNAME=
# DECODO_SECONDARY_PASSWORD=
# DECODO_SECONDARY_PORT_START=10001
# DECODO_SECONDARY_PORT_END=10010

# How the two endpoints are used (default: pool).
#   pool     = both port ranges form one rotating pool
#   failover = primary only; on a proxy error switch to the secondary for the rest of the run
# Precedence: --decodo-strategy > UI_SLEUTH_DECODO_STRATEGY > DECODO_STRATEGY > pool
# DECODO_STRATEGY=pool
# UI_SLEUTH_DECODO_STRATEGY=failover

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Generic proxies (read only with --proxy-env or UI_SLEUTH_PROXY_ENV=1)
#    PROXY_FILE takes priority over HTTPS_PROXY, which takes priority over HTTP_PROXY.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PROXY_FILE=proxies.txt
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://user:pass@proxy.example.com:8080
# HTTP_PROXY=http://user:pass@proxy.example.com:8080
